ok   every request gets a UUID, echoed and readable as request.request_id
ok   X-Request-Id from a trusted proxy is used only when well-formed
ok   RequestId.accepted? takes 1 to 255 bytes of letters, digits and hyphens
ok   every line logged in the request carries the id, the application's own included
ok   log_tags orders the request's tags; [] turns them off
ok   RequestId.scope_for puts the tags in log_tags order and marks the id's place
ok   the request path in the Scope is masked as the request log masks it
ok   the id is echoed on error responses
ok   the development front_app's nesting makes one id, one header and one Scope
ok   SslRedirect's redirect carries the id
ok   a streamed body is written under the request's Scope; nothing is left for the next request
ok   a raising request leaves no Scope behind on its thread
ok   a RequestId inside another keeps the outer id and adds no Scope of its own
ok   the controller and action are the request's where
ok   a controller run without a RequestId writes nothing to the shared empty Scope
ok   a request's 500 is reported once, with where, method and path
ok   no report for a 4xx or a rescue_from
ok   a reset link's token and the query never reach a report
ok   no params, headers, cookies or client address in an event
ok   a RequestId inside another reports once: the inner one, which saw the exception first
ok   an exception outside StandardError is reported too
ok   RequestId.where_for is the action, or request when none ran
